#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Read one page from a domain riffle is allowed to read.

    /usr/local/bin/riffle-reader     0755 root:root
    /etc/sudoers.d/riffle-reader     riffle -> (riffle-reader) NOPASSWD

ONE PAGE. NOT A CRAWLER.

No link following, no recursion, no site mirroring. A crawler violates most
of these sites' terms, gets the address blocked, and fills a library with
navigation chrome. This fetches a URL the agent gave a reason for, converts it
to text, and stops.

THE ALLOWLIST IS THE WHOLE SECURITY MODEL

Every domain below shares one property: a stranger cannot change what it says
without passing an editorial or institutional gate. Official documentation,
standards bodies, government agencies, peer-reviewed journals and their
preprint servers, curated encyclopedias. Nobody can post to dlmf.nist.gov.

That is not the same as "reputable". Stack Exchange is often right and is
excluded, because the risk was never that a page is wrong — it is that someone
can put text on it specifically for an agent to read.

Wikipedia is the one open exception, English only, and it is the weakest entry
here. Anything load-bearing from it gets checked against a second source.

REDIRECTS ARE CHECKED TOO

An allowlist that validates the requested URL and follows a 302 anywhere is
decorative. Redirects are followed manually, each hop re-checked, capped at
four.

IT RUNS AS ITS OWN USER

riffle-reader has no key, no sudo of its own, and no write access to anything
the agent owns. Same shape as riffle-build: the thing that touches the outside
world is not the thing that holds the credentials.
"""
import argparse
import html
import json
import re
import sys
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request

MAX_BYTES = 4 * 1024 * 1024
MAX_TEXT = 400_000
MAX_REDIRECTS = 4
TIMEOUT = 45
UA = ("riffle/1.0 (autonomous agent; 1f916.ai citizen; "
      "one page per request, no crawling)")

